<section class="mdt-hero">
    <span class="mdt-kicker">Independent medical opinion</span>
    <h1>When one specialist's answer isn't enough, put several of them in a room</h1>
    <p>An MDT Review takes your case to a multidisciplinary team &mdash; different specialists from the departments that matter for your condition, assessing it together, at the same time. Not a second opinion. A joint one.</p>
</section>

<main class="mdt-shell">

    <div class="mdt-section">
        <h2 class="mdt-section-title">Why a team beats a series of individual opinions</h2>
        <p class="mdt-section-sub">For complex cases, the hard part is rarely that a doctor is wrong. It's that each specialist only sees their own slice. A surgeon judges whether to operate; an oncologist judges which drug; a pathologist questions whether the diagnosis is even right. When they never speak to each other, nobody sees the whole picture &mdash; and you become the one carrying their opinions from room to room.</p>
        <div class="mdt-contrast">
            <div class="mdt-contrast-card">
                <h3>Second opinion</h3>
                <p>One specialist, working from your records, gives you their view. Valuable &mdash; but shaped by their own specialty, and delivered without the others in the room.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mdt-contrast-card is-ours">
                <span class="mdt-tag">What an MDT Review is</span>
                <h3>Multidisciplinary team</h3>
                <p>Several specialists from the relevant departments review your case together, in one session. They hear each other's reasoning, reconcile the disagreements between them, and produce one coordinated recommendation.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mdt-section">
        <h2 class="mdt-section-title">Is this the right step for you?</h2>
        <p class="mdt-section-sub">An MDT Review is not for everyone. It earns its cost when the decision itself is genuinely uncertain.</p>
        <div class="mdt-check">
            <div class="mdt-check-item"><strong>You have conflicting advice</strong>Two or more doctors have told you different things, and you don't know who to believe.</div>
            <div class="mdt-check-item"><strong>The treatment choice is close</strong>Surgery or chemotherapy? Operate now or watch and wait? Two reasonable paths, no clear winner.</div>
            <div class="mdt-check-item"><strong>The diagnosis itself is uncertain</strong>Rare tumours, unclear pathology, or a picture that doesn't quite fit any single label.</div>
            <div class="mdt-check-item"><strong>You've been told it's inoperable or untreatable</strong>Sometimes that conclusion is right. Sometimes it's the view of one department &mdash; and other departments have options.</div>
            <div class="mdt-check-item"><strong>Several conditions at once</strong>The treatment for one complicates the treatment for another, and someone has to weigh them jointly.</div>
            <div class="mdt-check-item"><strong>You want to know before you travel</strong>You're considering China and want an expert read on whether the trip is worth making.</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mdt-section">
        <h2 class="mdt-section-title">How it works</h2>
        <p class="mdt-section-sub">Four steps, from the records you already have to a written recommendation you can act on.</p>
        <div class="mdt-path">
            <div class="mdt-step">
                <div class="mdt-num">1</div>
                <h3>Case preparation</h3>
                <p>You send your records &mdash; scans, reports, pathology, treatment history, in any language. We organise them and distil everything into a Chinese case summary with a structured question list. This is the step that decides whether the panel can work efficiently, and it is the part most people underestimate.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mdt-step">
                <div class="mdt-num">2</div>
                <h3>Panel convened</h3>
                <p>At a partner hospital, we assemble a panel of specialists from the departments relevant to your condition &mdash; typically three to five consultants. The composition depends on your case; for a cancer case it would normally include medical oncology, surgery, radiotherapy and pathology.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mdt-step">
                <div class="mdt-num">3</div>
                <h3>Joint review</h3>
                <p>The panel meets on your case together. They review the records, compare interpretations, and work through the question list you supplied. Where they disagree, that disagreement is recorded rather than smoothed over &mdash; it is often the most useful part.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mdt-step">
                <div class="mdt-num">4</div>
                <h3>Report and consultation</h3>
                <p>You receive the written MDT report in English, then join a video consultation with the panel through medical interpretation, and finish with a call with us to walk through what each part means for your decision.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mdt-section">
        <h2 class="mdt-section-title">What you receive</h2>
        <div class="mdt-deliver">
            <ul>
                <li>A <strong>Chinese case summary and structured question list</strong>, prepared from your existing records</li>
                <li>A <strong>written MDT report in English</strong>: the panel's assessment, their recommended treatment approach, and the alternatives with their trade-offs</li>
                <li><strong>Points where the panel disagreed</strong>, stated plainly &mdash; not a falsely unanimous verdict</li>
                <li>What the hospital would still need from you in order to proceed, if you choose to</li>
                <li>A <strong>video consultation with the panel</strong>, with medical interpretation, so you can put your own questions to them directly</li>
                <li>A <strong>follow-up call with our team</strong> to walk through the report and answer what it leaves open</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mdt-exclude">
            <h3>What an MDT Review does not include</h3>
            <p>No coordination of any kind &mdash; no appointment booking, no visa invitation letter, no airport pickup, no in-country support. An MDT Review answers <em>what the right treatment is</em>. It does not get you there.</p>
            <p>If you want both, our <a href="pricing.html" style="color:#1e3c72;font-weight:700;">Full Journey Management</a> handles the travel and the coordination.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

