                {{ payButton(site.paypalButtons.mdtReview, 499, "mdt-review", "MDT Review", "Reserve at $499 &mdash; we&rsquo;ll send a payment link &#8594;") }}
            </div>
            <p class="mdt-buy-note">$469 is refundable if you cancel before the panel is convened. Independent of our coordination services &mdash; it neither includes them nor credits toward them.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mdt-section">
        <h2 class="mdt-section-title">Questions people ask</h2>
        <div class="mdt-faq">
            <details>
                <summary>Is this just a second opinion with a bigger name?</summary>
                <p>No, and the difference is not cosmetic. A second opinion is one specialist's read. An MDT is a joint session: the specialists hear each other, challenge each other's reasoning, and produce a single coordinated recommendation. For a straightforward case the two are close. For a genuinely uncertain one &mdash; conflicting advice, an unclear diagnosis, a borderline surgical decision &mdash; the joint version is doing something the individual one structurally cannot.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Do I need to travel to China for this?</summary>
                <p>No. The entire MDT Review runs on records you send us, plus the video consultation at the end. Many patients use it precisely to decide whether travelling is worth it at all. If the panel concludes China is not the right answer for your case, that is a useful result and we will tell you so.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>How many specialists will actually look at my case?</summary>
                <p>Typically three to five consultants, drawn from the departments relevant to your condition. The composition is set by your case, not by a fixed package &mdash; a cardiac case and a paediatric oncology case need different rooms. We tell you the proposed panel before the review takes place.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>What if the panel can't agree?</summary>
                <p>Then the report says so. Genuine disagreement between specialists is information, not a failure &mdash; it usually marks exactly where the difficult judgement lies, and you are better off knowing that than receiving a false consensus. We will also tell you where the weight of opinion sits.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does buying this discount a later Full Journey Management?</summary>
                <p>No. An MDT Review is a medical opinion; Full Journey Management is coordination. They are separate services, so an MDT purchase does not credit toward coordination.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>What do you need from me to start?</summary>
                <p>Your clinical summary, imaging (the actual DICOM files where possible, not only the reports), pathology reports, current medication list, previous treatment and surgical history, and any specialist consultations you have had. In any language &mdash; organising and translating them is part of what the fee covers.</p>
            </details>
        </div>
    </div>

